  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\n***UPDATE***\n\nLow level wind shear along a warm front over the area is\nallowing for increased tornado chances across eastern portions\nof the CWA. Several tornado warnings have been issued in the\npast 30 min, with 1 report of a tornado. Because of this, a\nTornado Watch has been issued.\n\n---\nComplicated setup today with many factors playing into\nthunderstorm development and severe storm chances. \n\nA strong shortwave trough moving into the western portion of the\narea is driving the development of scattered showers and \nstorms. Cirrus blow off from storms earlier over OH has limited \nthe instability over this region, allowing for a few weak \nstorms but mostly just periods of moderate to heavy rain. East \nof this area, we have seen more breaks in the clouds which has \nallowed stronger CAPE to develop, with values above 1000 j/kg \ncurrently growing from a N/S line bisecting the the Central \nFinger Lakes and to the east. 0-6 bulk shear values are \ncurrently in the 35-40kt range and should remain around this \nrange through the afternoon hours thanks to the strong shortwave\noverhead. With the current storms moving into a much more \nfavorable environment, we expect some of the storms to grow and \nbecome severe, with damaging winds and heavy rain and tornadoes\nwill be the main threats as the afternoon progresses. \nHodographs show tornadic signatures, with multiple swirls \nlocated along a line of storms moving into Otsego, Delaware and \nSullivan counties. Tornadoes will be possible as these storms \ndevelop through the afternoon hours.\n\n\nGuidance suggests we may have a brief lull in activity late \nthis afternoon and early evening. This would bring a potential \nfor some clearing that could reinforce some early evening \nsurface instability. At the same time, a 2nd shortwave \ncurrently over eastern MI is progged to track over the region. \nThis synoptic lift combined with surface instability and \nincreased 0-6km shear of 40-50kts would allow for more severe \nstorms to develop. The big question is will we get enough \nclearing to introduce sufficient CAPE. If we do not and the CAPE\nremains under 1000 j/kg, the amount of severe storms would be \nlower but severe chances will remain given the high shear values\nand synoptic lift provided by the transiting shortwave. \n\nHeavy rains will also accompany these storms, allowing for a\npotential for isolated flash flooding where multiple rounds of\nstorms impact an area. Currently, NEPA has a heightened chance\nfor isolated flash flooding with storms currently rolling\nthrough and the expected 2nd round of storms later this\nafternoon and evening. \n\nThe severe threat looks to end late this evening. Scattered rain\nshowers are going to linger through the night, especially north\nof the Southern Tier as the trough axis moves over the area. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nWith the trough lingering overhead into Wednesday, we expect\nsome scattered light rain showers to remain streaming across the\narea.  \n\nAfter the trough axis moves east of the area, cool Canadian air\nis expected to move into the region and plant itself through the\nrest of the work week as high pressure dominates the weather\npattern. Wednesday will be the coolest, with highs in the upper\n60s to mid 70s. We will see a slight warming trend through the \nrest of the week, with low to mid 80s by the weekend. Showers\nand storms may return Sunday or Monday as a trough tries to work\nits way into the region, but it will depend on if the ridge\noverhead weakens or flattens enough to allow for precip to move\nin.",
